{"k": "meta", "mode": "live", "dm": "frozen", "cmd": "check", "opt": "", "question": "", "model": "sonnet", "depth": "fast", "prompt": "Context: this is a classroom demonstration for UCL module IFTE0007 group coursework. The agent checks an academic forecast of the S&P 500 index built from frozen historical data. Nothing is traded and nothing here is investment advice.\n\nYou are the group's research agent. Goal: decide whether the group should submit the forecast record in this folder (S&P 500 official close on Fri 20 Nov 2026, S&P Dow Jones Indices, index points).\nThe brief's rules: every input must be available by the information cutoff, 12:00 UTC (07:00 New York) on the cutoff day; the calculation must be reproducible from the saved inputs; the forecast in the record is the one that is marked.\nMode: FROZEN. Use only the saved snapshot in this folder (the frozen inputs of the submitted forecast); there is no network access and no fetch tool. The information cutoff is Fri 13 Nov 2026 12:00 UTC (07:00 New York). Never change the forecast record. Run each command exactly as written, from the current folder (do not cd).\n\nYour tools (each takes a few seconds):\n- `python code/demo_tools.py --mode frozen record`: which record and data, what the cutoff removed, the brief's cutoff rule\n- `python code/demo_tools.py --mode frozen recompute`: a separate recalculation, MATCH or DIFFERS for each number\n- `python code/demo_tools.py --mode frozen check`: the package's independent second code path and leak test\n- `python code/demo_tools.py --mode frozen data spx` or `data es`: the raw observations either side of the cutoff\n- `python code/demo_tools.py --mode frozen explain <drift|futures|overnight|cutoff|forecast|check>`: how a number is defined and where in the code\n- `python code/demo_tools.py --mode frozen whatif <change> [<change> ...]` with changes such as drift=mean, drift=median:2010, drift=0.3%, drift=none, futures=none, futures=06:30, overnight=07:00, close=7560.25\n- reading files in this folder.\n\nA good check establishes three things: which record and data are being checked and that nothing after the cutoff was used (or, in Full mode, exactly what came after it); that the forecast can be reproduced independently; and whether anything looks wrong, and what it would mean. You decide how to establish them.\nStart with one line \"Plan:\" naming the tools you will use, in order, and why. All data is the frozen snapshot already in this folder; there is nothing to download. Use at most five tool calls. Change your plan if a result surprises you, and say so.\nNarrate for an audience. Before each tool call write one line starting \"Checking:\" (what and why). If you expect a particular result, first write \"Expect:\" with a rough number. After each call write \"Found:\" (the key number), then \"Expectation:\" if you made one (right or wrong), then \"Decision:\" (what you will do next and why). If anything does not match, say what it could mean before you decide. Each line under 25 words. No tables, no headings, no bold.\nFinish with one line \"Recommendation:\" (submit or not, and why) and one line \"Would change my mind:\" (one concrete thing). You recommend; the group decides.", "drill": null, "label": "13 Nov 15:35 UTC", "file": "20261113-153533_check.jsonl", "t": 0.0}
